{"units":[
{"id":"pkg/router/aggregate_discovery.go:newDiscoveredAggregateBackend","summary":"The aggregate backend's ReverseProxy now sets FlushInterval to -1, so every write is flushed to the client immediately. This changes streaming behavior for responses that carry a Content-Length.","issues":[],"bucket":"human","change_kind":"behavior","headline":"Aggregate proxy now flushes after every write","confidence":0.8,"reason":"Changes ReverseProxy flushing, which affects streaming and buffering of all aggregated responses.","risk_signals":["streaming behavior changed","proxy flush semantics"],"focus":["Responses with Content-Length are now flushed per write, which may cost throughput on large bodies"]},
{"id":"pkg/router/forward.go:NewForwardBackend","summary":"The forward backend's ReverseProxy now sets FlushInterval to -1, so every write is flushed immediately. Watch events are no longer at the mercy of the Content-Length heuristic.","issues":[],"bucket":"human","change_kind":"behavior","headline":"Forward proxy now flushes after every write","confidence":0.8,"reason":"Changes ReverseProxy flushing, which affects streaming and buffering of all forwarded responses.","risk_signals":["streaming behavior changed","proxy flush semantics"],"focus":["Responses with Content-Length are now flushed per write, which may cost throughput on large bodies"]},
{"id":"pkg/router/proxy_errors.go:const streamingFlushInterval","summary":"Adds a constant of -1 that is passed to ReverseProxy.FlushInterval in the forward, aggregate and single-tenant proxies. It has no effect on its own. The comment's claim holds for ReverseProxy, and in-process backends are not proxies.","issues":[],"bucket":"skim","change_kind":"config","headline":"New streamingFlushInterval constant set to -1","confidence":0.8,"reason":"A constant definition only. The behavior change is in the units that use it.","risk_signals":[],"focus":[]},
{"id":"pkg/router/st_fallback.go:type singleTenantFallback","summary":"The breakers cache now stores *groupBreaker, an alias for gobreaker's two-step breaker, instead of the one-step breaker. This is a type change that follows the PR's breaker rework.","issues":[],"bucket":"skim","change_kind":"refactor","headline":"ST fallback breaker cache holds two-step breakers","confidence":0.7,"reason":"A field type change that follows the alias, with no logic change here. The behavior change is in breaker.go.","risk_signals":[],"focus":[]},
{"id":"pkg/router/st_fallback.go:newSingleTenantFallback","summary":"The LRU cache for breakers is constructed with the *groupBreaker element type. Only the type parameter changes.","issues":[],"bucket":"skim","change_kind":"refactor","headline":"Breaker LRU now built with groupBreaker type","confidence":0.8,"reason":"Only the generic type argument changed, and it matches the field type.","risk_signals":[],"focus":[]},
{"id":"pkg/router/st_fallback.go:(*singleTenantFallback).forward","summary":"The single-tenant fallback's ReverseProxy now sets FlushInterval to -1, so it flushes after every write. Watches through the ST fallback stream regardless of Content-Length.","issues":[],"bucket":"human","change_kind":"behavior","headline":"ST fallback proxy now flushes after every write","confidence":0.8,"reason":"Changes ReverseProxy flushing, which affects streaming of all single-tenant fallback responses.","risk_signals":["streaming behavior changed","proxy flush semantics"],"focus":["Responses with Content-Length are now flushed per write, which may cost throughput on large bodies"]},
{"id":"pkg/router/st_fallback.go:(*singleTenantFallback).breakerForDestination","summary":"The return type changes to *groupBreaker, matching the cache's new element type. The key and locking logic are unchanged.","issues":[],"bucket":"skim","change_kind":"refactor","headline":"breakerForDestination returns groupBreaker type","confidence":0.75,"reason":"A signature type change that follows the alias, with the body untouched. Callers' breaker usage is in other units.","risk_signals":[],"focus":[]}
]}

{"units":[
{"id":"pkg/router/breaker.go:type statusRecorder","summary":"statusRecorder gains an optional onStatus callback field used to report the breaker outcome when the status is written. Unexported struct, but it drives breaker behavior.","issues":[],"bucket":"skim","change_kind":"behavior","headline":"statusRecorder gets optional onStatus callback field","confidence":0.7,"reason":"Unexported field that does nothing until WriteHeader invokes it; the behavioral effect is reviewed in the WriteHeader and serveThroughBreaker units.","risk_signals":[],"focus":[]},
{"id":"pkg/router/breaker.go:(*statusRecorder).WriteHeader","summary":"WriteHeader now invokes onStatus with the final status as soon as it is recorded. This is what lets the breaker learn the outcome before the body ends.","issues":[],"bucket":"human","change_kind":"behavior","headline":"WriteHeader reports status to breaker callback immediately","confidence":0.8,"reason":"Calls a callback in the response write path that decides circuit breaker accounting, so timing of outcome reporting changes.","risk_signals":["breaker accounting timing","callback in response path"],"focus":["callback runs synchronously inside WriteHeader, so it must not block or panic","repeated WriteHeader calls with code >= 200 invoke the callback again; only the sync.Once in the caller makes it once"]},
{"id":"pkg/router/breaker.go:type groupBreaker","summary":"Adds a type alias groupBreaker for gobreaker's TwoStepCircuitBreaker so call sites share one breaker type. The alias is used across the router and the ST fallback.","issues":[],"bucket":"skim","change_kind":"refactor","headline":"Add groupBreaker alias for two-step circuit breaker","confidence":0.75,"reason":"A type alias only; the behavior change comes from the constructor and serveThroughBreaker units.","risk_signals":[],"focus":[]},
{"id":"pkg/router/breaker.go:newGroupBreaker","summary":"The per-group breaker constructor now builds a two-step breaker instead of a one-shot Execute breaker, with the same settings. All users, including the ST fallback, now get the two-step API.","issues":[],"bucket":"human","change_kind":"behavior","headline":"Group breakers are now gobreaker two-step breakers","confidence":0.8,"reason":"Changes the breaker implementation used in production for every group and ST destination.","risk_signals":["circuit breaker semantics"],"focus":["every caller of the returned breaker, including the ST fallback forward path, uses Allow and done correctly","IsExcluded settings still apply to errors passed to done"]},
{"id":"pkg/router/breaker.go:serveThroughBreaker","summary":"The request now goes through cb.Allow and reports the outcome via done as soon as the response status is written, with a deferred fallback and panic handling guarded by sync.Once. This stops a streaming watch from holding the half-open trial slot.","issues":[],"bucket":"human","change_kind":"behavior","headline":"Breaker outcome reported at status write, not handler return","confidence":0.9,"reason":"Rewrites concurrency and error accounting for all proxied requests, with panic recovery and re-panic.","risk_signals":["concurrency","error handling changed","panic recovery and re-panic","circuit breaker semantics"],"focus":["every path after Allow succeeds calls done exactly once, including panic and http.ErrAbortHandler","status-time outcome uses the proxy failure already recorded by the error handler before WriteHeader","a 2xx watch that later fails mid-stream no longer counts against the backend","the 503 on Allow error is written through the traced writer"]},
{"id":"pkg/router/breaker_test.go:withGroupHandlerAndBreaker","summary":"Test helper signature switched to the groupBreaker alias to match production types.","issues":[],"bucket":"skim","change_kind":"test","headline":"Test helper takes groupBreaker type","confidence":0.9,"reason":"Test-only type change that follows the production alias.","risk_signals":[],"focus":[]},
{"id":"pkg/router/breaker_test.go:TestHandleFuncBreakerHalfOpenCapRejectsConcurrentTrial","summary":"The test now builds a two-step breaker to match the new production type. Assertions are unchanged.","issues":[],"bucket":"skim","change_kind":"test","headline":"Half-open cap test uses two-step breaker","confidence":0.88,"reason":"Test-only constructor swap with no expectations changed.","risk_signals":[],"focus":[]},
{"id":"pkg/router/breaker_test.go:tripBreaker","summary":"The helper trips the breaker via Allow and done with a forced failure instead of Execute. It silently does nothing if Allow errors.","issues":[],"bucket":"skim","change_kind":"test","headline":"tripBreaker uses Allow/done instead of Execute","confidence":0.88,"reason":"Test-only helper adapted to the two-step API, same effect of one forced failure.","risk_signals":[],"focus":[]}
]}

{"units":[
{"id":"pkg/router/breaker_test.go:TestReconcileUnchangedKeyPreservesBreakerState","summary":"Test now builds a two-step breaker to match the new groupBreaker type. Assertions are unchanged, so a line-by-line review can be skipped.","issues":[],"bucket":"skim","change_kind":"test","headline":"Reconcile test uses two-step circuit breaker","confidence":0.9,"reason":"Test-only type swap to match the production breaker type; no assertions removed or changed.","risk_signals":[],"focus":[]},
{"id":"pkg/router/breaker_test.go:TestReconcileChangedKeyResetsBreaker","summary":"Test now builds the old breaker as a two-step breaker to match the new groupBreaker type. Assertions are unchanged, so a line-by-line review can be skipped.","issues":[],"bucket":"skim","change_kind":"test","headline":"Changed-key reconcile test uses two-step breaker","confidence":0.9,"reason":"Test-only type swap to match the production breaker type; no assertions removed or changed.","risk_signals":[],"focus":[]},
{"id":"pkg/router/router.go:type handlerEntry","summary":"handlerEntry now holds a groupBreaker (two-step) and a per-handler watches context with its cancel func. Reconcile ends that context when the handler is replaced or removed, so watches on it terminate.","issues":[],"bucket":"human","change_kind":"behavior","headline":"handlerEntry gains two-step breaker and per-handler watch context","confidence":0.85,"reason":"Struct field changes and a new cancellation lifecycle that decides when in-flight watches are terminated.","risk_signals":["struct fields changed","concurrency","context cancellation lifecycle"],"focus":["Entries built directly in tests or elsewhere without watches/endWatches (nil context) are handled by serveWatch","Every replacement or removal path cancels the old entry's context exactly once"]},
{"id":"pkg/router/router.go:newHandlerEntry","summary":"New constructor creates a handler entry with a fresh closed breaker and a cancelable watch context. It replaces the inline struct literal and in-place mutation in reconcile.","issues":[],"bucket":"human","change_kind":"behavior","headline":"New constructor gives each handler its own cancelable watch context","confidence":0.8,"reason":"Creates a context per entry whose cancellation ends client watches; a leak or missed cancel changes runtime behavior.","risk_signals":["concurrency","resource lifecycle (context cancel)"],"focus":["Each created entry's endWatches is eventually called, or the context leaks","Fresh breaker per entry matches the prior reset-on-change semantics"]},
{"id":"pkg/router/router.go:type servingEntry","summary":"The published serving entry now carries the groupBreaker type and the handler's watches context, so request-time code can bind a watch to its backend's lifetime.","issues":[],"bucket":"human","change_kind":"behavior","headline":"servingEntry carries watch context and two-step breaker","confidence":0.8,"reason":"Struct field added and retyped; it feeds the request path that decides when watches end.","risk_signals":["struct fields changed","concurrency"],"focus":["Other constructors of servingEntry set watches or tolerate nil","Snapshot readers see a watches context consistent with the handler they serve"]},
{"id":"pkg/router/router.go:NewGrafanaRouter","summary":"The router now creates a router-wide watches context at construction. It is cancelled by closeWatches at service stop so streaming watches end and shutdown does not wait on them.","issues":[],"bucket":"human","change_kind":"behavior","headline":"Router creates a router-wide watch context at construction","confidence":0.8,"reason":"Introduces the shutdown cancellation scope for long-lived watches.","risk_signals":["concurrency","shutdown behavior"],"focus":["Watches started after closeWatches has run end immediately rather than hanging shutdown","Routers constructed in tests or other callers still get a live context"]},
{"id":"pkg/router/router.go:(*GrafanaRouter).reconcile","summary":"Changed groups are now replaced with a new handlerEntry rather than mutated in place. Replaced and removed entries are collected and their watch contexts cancelled after publish, so clients re-watch against the new backend.","issues":[],"bucket":"human","change_kind":"behavior","headline":"Reconcile replaces changed entries and ends their watches after publish","confidence":0.85,"reason":"Changes the replace/remove semantics and terminates in-flight client watches on backend change.","risk_signals":["concurrency","behavior on backend change","long-lived request termination"],"focus":["Order: publish before endWatches so a re-watching client sees the new entry","Anything holding the old *handlerEntry pointer after the replacement instead of mutation","Early-return and error paths in reconcile that skip cancelling retired entries"]},
{"id":"pkg/router/router.go:(*GrafanaRouter).publish","summary":"The published servingEntry now includes the handler's watches context, so HandleFunc can scope a watch to its backend.","issues":[],"bucket":"human","change_kind":"behavior","headline":"publish copies watch context into serving snapshot","confidence":0.8,"reason":"Propagates the cancellation context that controls watch lifetime into the request path.","risk_signals":["concurrency"],"focus":["Entries with a nil watches context (test-built handlerEntry) don't crash serveWatch"]}
]}

